From 19552d6912a7835be503891c7d7e04d4c00864f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaishil-Patel <jaishilpatel1105@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 09:50:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] Add per-sensor switches, a spawn pose, and
 headless/no-MoveIt launch options

All four exist for the RL navigation task (proposal Task 4).

Per-sensor switches (sensor_depth_camera, sensor_front_lidar, sensor_imu, each gated
by the existing `sensors`). `sensors` was all-or-nothing, which made "what does the
depth camera actually cost?" unanswerable: under LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 every
rendered sensor is CPU-rasterised and the cost is dominated by the 320x240 depth
camera, not the 15-ray lidar. Turning depth off alone is also the mechanism for the
lidar-only fallback, which deviates from the depth-based modality S4.4.2 fixes and so
has to be an explicit, visible choice rather than a side effect.

Spawn pose (spawn_x/y/z/yaw -> `create -x -y -z -Y`). mesh_import.py's
--origin-mode floor-min-corner puts the world origin in a room corner, so the default
(0,0) spawn wedges Pepper into two walls and she tips over. The corner origin is the
right convention for mapping real poses onto simulated ones; the spawn pose is what
should move, and until now it could not.

headless (Ignition `-s`, server only) and enable_moveit. Training does not want a GUI,
and a throughput measurement taken with one attached measures the GUI. move_group
plans for the arms while the navigation task drives the base, so on six cores it is
pure contention for the cores the software-rendered camera needs.

Also fixes a latent trap while editing: XML comments may not contain a double hyphen,
and the prose style used throughout this project's docs is full of them.
